  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You want a teammate to start using a shared calendar for deadlines. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Show how missed dates have caused problems and how the calendar can prevent them$qb$, $qb$Explain how the shared calendar works and suggest a short trial$qb$, $qb$Tell them everyone should use the same calendar$qb$, $qb$Add their deadlines to the calendar yourself$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to make calendar use compulsory$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A coworker asks for your work by noon, but you can reliably deliver it at 2 PM. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Explain the constraint and agree whether 2 PM works or the scope should change$qb$, $qb$Offer 2 PM and ask what part is needed most urgently$qb$, $qb$Promise noon and try to finish faster$qb$, $qb$Refuse the request because noon is impossible$qb$, $qb$Say 'I'll try' without giving a realistic time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague misunderstands the phrase 'end of day' because your teams work in different time zones. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Use a specific time and time zone in future messages$qb$, $qb$Clarify the exact time for this request$qb$, $qb$Use 'end of day' but add your city name$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague to convert the time themselves$qb$, $qb$Keep using the phrase because it is common at work$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate criticizes your work in a group message and you disagree with the criticism. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the concern, check the facts, and discuss the issue without attacking the person$qb$, $qb$Ask for the specific example behind the criticism$qb$, $qb$Reply with evidence showing why they are wrong$qb$, $qb$Move the disagreement to a larger group$qb$, $qb$Ignore the message and stop cooperating$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need a colleague to correct a file before a client call. Which message is best?$qb$, $qb$Please correct rows 12–15 by 2 PM and let me know when done; the 3 PM client call uses this file$qb$, $qb$Please correct rows 12–15 by 2 PM for the client call$qb$, $qb$Please correct the file before the client call$qb$, $qb$There are errors in the file that need attention$qb$, $qb$Please fix this urgently$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate explains a delay and mentions both a technical problem and a late input. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Summarize both factors and ask which one is still blocking progress$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge both factors and ask what help is needed now$qb$, $qb$Focus on the technical problem because it sounds more serious$qb$, $qb$Tell them how to avoid delays next time$qb$, $qb$Assume the late input was the real cause$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You want a colleague to adopt a short review step before sending customer emails. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Show an example of an avoidable error and how the review step would catch it$qb$, $qb$Explain the review step and ask them to try it for a week$qb$, $qb$Tell them careful people always review emails$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to add the step to their goals$qb$, $qb$Review their emails yourself instead$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague wants your draft today, but you need until tomorrow to check important figures. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Explain the quality risk and agree whether they need an unchecked draft today or a checked version tomorrow$qb$, $qb$Offer an early draft today and a checked version tomorrow$qb$, $qb$Send the draft today without explaining the unchecked figures$qb$, $qb$Refuse to share anything until tomorrow$qb$, $qb$Promise a checked version today even though it is unrealistic$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A global team message uses several local jokes that some colleagues do not understand. What is the best improvement?$qb$, $qb$Replace the jokes with clear language that everyone can understand$qb$, $qb$Keep one joke but explain its meaning$qb$, $qb$Send separate explanations to colleagues who ask$qb$, $qb$Assume people will understand from context$qb$, $qb$Use more local jokes so the message feels friendly$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A coworker says you caused a delay, but the records show several factors contributed. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Review the facts together, acknowledge your part, and agree what should change$qb$, $qb$Explain your part and ask them to review the other factors$qb$, $qb$Show the records to prove you were not mainly responsible$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to defend you$qb$, $qb$Blame the other factors instead$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A customer asks when an issue will be fixed, but the exact time is not known. Which reply is clearest?$qb$, $qb$We are checking the cause now; I will update you by 2 PM even if the fix is still in progress$qb$, $qb$We are working on it and I will give you another update by 2 PM$qb$, $qb$We expect progress today and will keep you informed$qb$, $qb$It should be fixed soon$qb$, $qb$We do not know$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague gives you instructions, then says, 'The last step is the most important.' What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Confirm what the last step requires and why it matters before starting$qb$, $qb$Repeat the last step in your own words and check it$qb$, $qb$Write down the last step carefully$qb$, $qb$Focus only on the last step and ignore the earlier ones$qb$, $qb$Start immediately without checking your understanding$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You want a teammate to use a simple naming rule because files are often hard to find. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Show the time lost finding files and demonstrate how the naming rule makes retrieval easier$qb$, $qb$Explain the naming rule and ask them to try it on new files$qb$, $qb$Tell them organized teams use naming rules$qb$, $qb$Rename their files yourself$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to enforce the rule$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague needs a report today, but you can provide either a quick summary now or the full report tomorrow. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask what decision they need to make and agree which option best meets that need$qb$, $qb$Offer the summary today and full report tomorrow$qb$, $qb$Send the summary today without discussing their need$qb$, $qb$Refuse because the full report is not ready$qb$, $qb$Promise the full report today$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague says your email sounds too direct in their work culture. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask what wording felt too direct and adjust the message while keeping the action clear$qb$, $qb$Thank them and soften the wording where appropriate$qb$, $qb$Explain that you always write directly$qb$, $qb$Use very indirect language in all future emails$qb$, $qb$Ignore the comment because the meaning was clear$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two teammates disagree about a task and both keep repeating their positions. What is the best next step?$qb$, $qb$Identify the shared outcome and compare the reasons behind each option$qb$, $qb$Summarize both positions and ask what each person needs$qb$, $qb$Ask them to vote on the two options$qb$, $qb$Choose the view of the more experienced teammate$qb$, $qb$End the discussion without a decision$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R1-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Which communication strategy is most effective when briefing cross-departmental stakeholders on a complex workflow update?$qb$, $qb$Structuring the update around core operational outcomes, providing validated timeline milestones, and offering a summary sheet for quick reference.$qb$, $qb$Sending a detailed narrative document explaining every procedural step to ensure full technical transparency across all teams.$qb$, $qb$Delivering a brief verbal summary during a routine status call and taking note of any immediate questions raised.$qb$, $qb$Forwarding raw workflow documentation files to stakeholders and asking them to review relevant sections independently.$qb$, $qb$Delaying the briefing until all secondary project sub-tasks are fully completed to avoid sharing partial updates.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R1-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$What is the primary indicator of active listening during a feedback session with a key internal partner?$qb$, $qb$Paraphrasing the speaker's main points in your own words to verify mutual understanding before proposing solutions.$qb$, $qb$Taking exhaustive written notes continuously while the partner speaks to capture every spoken word.$qb$, $qb$Nodding attentively throughout the discussion and thanking the partner for sharing their perspective.$qb$, $qb$Offering immediate explanations for highlighted operational gaps to clarify context as soon as issues are raised.$qb$, $qb$Remaining silent throughout the conversation and waiting to address raised points in a follow-up email.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R1-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$How can a project coordinator best persuade reluctant peer leads to adopt a new inter-departmental request tool?$qb$, $qb$Demonstrating how the tool reduces administrative tracking effort for their specific teams and offering setup support.$qb$, $qb$Citing official management guidelines that mandate the adoption of standardized tracking software.$qb$, $qb$Highlighting the operational benefits that your own department will gain once all teams adopt the tool.$qb$, $qb$Asking department managers to issue formal reminders regarding compliance during upcoming team meetings.$qb$, $qb$Adopting the tool within your team only and waiting for peer leads to request access on their own schedule.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R1-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$When negotiating project scope adjustments with a client under tight budget limits, what represents the most effective approach?$qb$, $qb$Evaluating core underlying priorities to rebalance deliverable scope while protecting project margin boundaries.$qb$, $qb$Offering a standard percentage price reduction immediately to demonstrate goodwill and secure contract agreement.$qb$, $qb$Reiterating standard rate card policies firmly and advising the client that adjustments are unavailable.$qb$, $qb$Escalating the negotiation directly to executive leadership to determine if margin exceptions can be granted.$qb$, $qb$Accepting all requested scope additions at current pricing to maintain positive client sentiment.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R1-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$What is the most effective method to ensure clear task execution when working with international remote collaborators?$qb$, $qb$Standardizing written communications with plain language, bulleted action items, and explicit verification check-ins.$qb$, $qb$Relying on frequent informal video calls to discuss task expectations and resolve misunderstandings verbally.$qb$, $qb$Sending automated task reminders at higher frequencies to ensure project deadlines remain top of mind.$qb$, $qb$Reassigning complex analytical tasks to local team members while leaving routine steps with global collaborators.$qb$, $qb$Using standard region-specific idioms and assuming collaborators will seek clarification if context is unclear.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R1-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$What is the recommended first step when resolving a resource allocation disagreement between two team members?$qb$, $qb$Facilitating a private alignment conversation to re-establish shared project goals and co-create clear task ownership.$qb$, $qb$Reassigning conflicting resources personally to eliminate immediate operational friction and protect project schedules.$qb$, $qb$Advising both team members to resolve their differences professionally before the upcoming project deadline.$qb$, $qb$Escalating the conflict to department managers so task responsibilities can be officially dictated.$qb$, $qb$Allocating contested resources strictly based on employee team seniority to settle the debate rapidly.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need to update a project group after a delay changes both the delivery date and another team's work. Which message is best?$qb$, $qb$State what changed, the new date, the impact on dependent work, and the action each person now needs to take$qb$, $qb$State the new date and explain which dependent work is affected$qb$, $qb$Explain the reason for the delay and provide the revised date$qb$, $qb$Say the schedule has changed and more detail will follow$qb$, $qb$Wait until every consequence is known before sending an update$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague describes a recurring problem, but their proposed solution does not seem to address the cause. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Summarize the problem you heard, ask questions about the cause, and only then discuss possible solutions$qb$, $qb$Check your understanding of the problem and ask what led them to their proposed solution$qb$, $qb$Explain why their proposed solution may not work$qb$, $qb$Suggest a better solution based on your experience$qb$, $qb$Let them implement their solution because they know the problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need another team to change a handoff practice that saves them time but creates repeated rework for your team. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Show the end-to-end impact, understand their constraint, and propose a change that improves the handoff for both teams$qb$, $qb$Share evidence of the rework and ask them to adjust the handoff$qb$, $qb$Explain how much extra work their current practice creates for your team$qb$, $qb$Escalate the issue to both managers so the change is enforced$qb$, $qb$Create a workaround within your team and stop asking them to change$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A stakeholder wants an earlier deadline without reducing scope, while your team has a fixed capacity. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Clarify what matters most, make the time-scope-capacity trade-offs visible, and agree a realistic option$qb$, $qb$Offer an earlier delivery for the highest-priority part and the rest later$qb$, $qb$Accept the deadline and ask the team to find a way$qb$, $qb$Reject the request because capacity is fixed$qb$, $qb$Agree to the date but quietly reduce lower-priority work$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two teams in different countries interpret the phrase 'urgent' differently, causing repeated confusion. What is the best improvement?$qb$, $qb$Agree clear response-time definitions and use specific deadlines for important requests$qb$, $qb$Use exact dates and times whenever a request is important$qb$, $qb$Explain how your team normally uses the word 'urgent'$qb$, $qb$Ask each team to adapt to the other team's style$qb$, $qb$Keep using the term and resolve misunderstandings when they occur$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two colleagues disagree about a failed handoff and each has evidence supporting part of their position. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Separate agreed facts from disputed points, identify the process gap, and agree how the next handoff will work$qb$, $qb$Let each person present their evidence and summarize the areas of agreement$qb$, $qb$Decide which person's evidence is stronger and close the discussion$qb$, $qb$Ask their managers to determine responsibility$qb$, $qb$Avoid assigning any responsibility so the relationship stays positive$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R3-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A manager asks, 'Will the file be ready today?' You know one check is still pending. The update also affects a teammate's dependent deadline. Which reply is best?$qb$, $qb$The file should be ready by 5 PM after I complete the final check; I will alert you if that changes$qb$, $qb$I expect to send it today after one remaining check$qb$, $qb$It is almost ready and should be sent today$qb$, $qb$Yes, it will probably be ready$qb$, $qb$Yes$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R3-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague says a task is 'not working' but gives no details. The explanation will determine what action another person takes next. What is the best first response?$qb$, $qb$Ask what they expected, what happened, and when the problem started$qb$, $qb$Ask them to show you the problem$qb$, $qb$Suggest the most common fix$qb$, $qb$Tell them to try the task again$qb$, $qb$Assume you know what they mean$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R3-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You want your team to start recording one extra field because it will reduce follow-up questions. The change will be easier to adopt only if the colleague sees a practical benefit. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Show the repeated follow-up problem and how the extra field would prevent it$qb$, $qb$Explain the new field and ask the team to try it$qb$, $qb$Tell the team the extra field is good practice$qb$, $qb$Add the field and tell everyone to use it$qb$, $qb$Record it yourself and leave others unchanged$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R3-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate needs your help this afternoon, but you have only 30 minutes free. Changing the timing will also affect another commitment. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Agree what can realistically be achieved in 30 minutes and focus on that$qb$, $qb$Offer the 30 minutes and ask what is most urgent$qb$, $qb$Help until your next task becomes urgent$qb$, $qb$Say you are too busy to help$qb$, $qb$Promise more time than you have$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R3-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks you to explain a local abbreviation they have not seen before. The message will be read by colleagues in more than one location. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Explain the full term and what it means in this work context$qb$, $qb$Give the full form and a short example$qb$, $qb$Tell them everyone learns it after some time$qb$, $qb$Send them an old message that uses it$qb$, $qb$Tell them to search for it online$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R3-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You disagree with a teammate about who was responsible for an error. The working relationship must continue after the disagreement is resolved. What is the best way to move forward?$qb$, $qb$Check the facts, fix the immediate issue, and clarify responsibility without personal blame$qb$, $qb$Review what each person was expected to do$qb$, $qb$Explain why you believe the teammate was responsible$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to decide who was at fault$qb$, $qb$Stop cooperating until the issue is settled$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A client asks for an update while two internal teams are still resolving different parts of the issue. Which response is best?$qb$, $qb$Give the confirmed status, separate what is still unresolved, name the next owner or action, and commit to the next update time$qb$, $qb$Share the confirmed status and say which part is still being checked$qb$, $qb$Explain that two teams are working on the issue and more detail will follow$qb$, $qb$Wait until both teams agree on the full answer$qb$, $qb$Send each team's separate update without combining them$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague says a process is 'unfair' and gives several examples from recent weeks. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Reflect the pattern you heard, ask what outcome feels unfair, and clarify the facts before judging the process$qb$, $qb$Summarize the examples and ask which one best represents the problem$qb$, $qb$Explain why the process was designed that way$qb$, $qb$Offer a solution to the most recent example$qb$, $qb$Tell them to raise the concern formally instead$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Another team is hesitant to adopt your proposed handoff change because they expect extra effort. Which approach is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge their concern, show the shared benefit with evidence, and design the change with them so the effort is practical$qb$, $qb$Show the evidence and ask them to trial the change for a short period$qb$, $qb$Explain that the current handoff creates avoidable work for your team$qb$, $qb$Escalate the change because cross-team consistency is important$qb$, $qb$Implement a workaround without changing their handoff$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A stakeholder wants both faster delivery and more review steps, but the team cannot provide both with current capacity. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the outcome they value most, show the trade-off, and agree the combination of timing and review that best meets the need$qb$, $qb$Offer two realistic options and ask them to choose$qb$, $qb$Keep the review steps and extend the deadline without discussion$qb$, $qb$Remove a review step so the deadline can be met$qb$, $qb$Promise both and ask the team to absorb the extra work$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A global project uses technical shorthand that is clear to specialists but confusing to several business users. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Use necessary terms consistently, explain them once in plain language, and keep key actions understandable to all readers$qb$, $qb$Add a short glossary and use simpler wording in important messages$qb$, $qb$Continue using the shorthand and answer questions when they arise$qb$, $qb$Replace all technical terms even when precision is lost$qb$, $qb$Send separate technical and non-technical versions without common wording$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two colleagues disagree about a decision and are now questioning each other's motives. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Bring the discussion back to evidence, agreed goals, and decision criteria, while stopping personal assumptions$qb$, $qb$Ask each person to explain the facts behind their position and summarize common ground$qb$, $qb$Choose the stronger proposal and end the discussion quickly$qb$, $qb$Ask both people to avoid each other until emotions settle$qb$, $qb$Escalate the disagreement before trying to resolve it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R6-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A customer asks when an issue will be fixed, but the exact time is not known. The answer will shape the customer's next action and another team's workload. Which reply is clearest?$qb$, $qb$We are checking the cause now; I will update you by 2 PM even if the fix is still in progress$qb$, $qb$We are working on it and I will give you another update by 2 PM$qb$, $qb$We expect progress today and will keep you informed$qb$, $qb$It should be fixed soon$qb$, $qb$We do not know$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R6-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague gives you instructions, then says, 'The last step is the most important.' You are expected to use the handover independently after this discussion. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Confirm what the last step requires and why it matters before starting$qb$, $qb$Repeat the last step in your own words and check it$qb$, $qb$Write down the last step carefully$qb$, $qb$Focus only on the last step and ignore the earlier ones$qb$, $qb$Start immediately without checking your understanding$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R6-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You want a teammate to use a simple naming rule because files are often hard to find. Adoption depends on voluntary cooperation, not formal authority. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Show the time lost finding files and demonstrate how the naming rule makes retrieval easier$qb$, $qb$Explain the naming rule and ask them to try it on new files$qb$, $qb$Tell them organized teams use naming rules$qb$, $qb$Rename their files yourself$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to enforce the rule$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R6-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague needs a report today, but you can provide either a quick summary now or the full report tomorrow. The choice affects both today's need and tomorrow's committed work. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask what decision they need to make and agree which option best meets that need$qb$, $qb$Offer the summary today and full report tomorrow$qb$, $qb$Send the summary today without discussing their need$qb$, $qb$Refuse because the full report is not ready$qb$, $qb$Promise the full report today$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R6-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague says your email sounds too direct in their work culture. The same wording will be reused with colleagues in several countries. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask what wording felt too direct and adjust the message while keeping the action clear$qb$, $qb$Thank them and soften the wording where appropriate$qb$, $qb$Explain that you always write directly$qb$, $qb$Use very indirect language in all future emails$qb$, $qb$Ignore the comment because the meaning was clear$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R6-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two teammates disagree about a task and both keep repeating their positions. The disagreement is beginning to affect delivery, not only the relationship. What is the best next step?$qb$, $qb$Identify the shared outcome and compare the reasons behind each option$qb$, $qb$Summarize both positions and ask what each person needs$qb$, $qb$Ask them to vote on the two options$qb$, $qb$Choose the view of the more experienced teammate$qb$, $qb$End the discussion without a decision$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R7-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need a colleague to correct a file before a client call. The message will also affect a dependent task owned by another person. Which message is best?$qb$, $qb$Please correct rows 12–15 by 2 PM and let me know when done; the 3 PM client call uses this file$qb$, $qb$Please correct rows 12–15 by 2 PM for the client call$qb$, $qb$Please correct the file before the client call$qb$, $qb$There are errors in the file that need attention$qb$, $qb$Please fix this urgently$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R7-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate explains a delay and mentions both a technical problem and a late input. You will need to act on the handover without asking the speaker again. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Summarize both factors and ask which one is still blocking progress$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge both factors and ask what help is needed now$qb$, $qb$Focus on the technical problem because it sounds more serious$qb$, $qb$Tell them how to avoid delays next time$qb$, $qb$Assume the late input was the real cause$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R7-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You want a colleague to adopt a short review step before sending customer emails. The colleague has discretion over whether to adopt the practice. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Show an example of an avoidable error and how the review step would catch it$qb$, $qb$Explain the review step and ask them to try it for a week$qb$, $qb$Tell them careful people always review emails$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to add the step to their goals$qb$, $qb$Review their emails yourself instead$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R7-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague wants your draft today, but you need until tomorrow to check important figures. Changing the delivery choice will affect another commitment. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Explain the quality risk and agree whether they need an unchecked draft today or a checked version tomorrow$qb$, $qb$Offer an early draft today and a checked version tomorrow$qb$, $qb$Send the draft today without explaining the unchecked figures$qb$, $qb$Refuse to share anything until tomorrow$qb$, $qb$Promise a checked version today even though it is unrealistic$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R7-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A global team message uses several local jokes that some colleagues do not understand. The wording will be reused by colleagues in different locations. What is the best improvement?$qb$, $qb$Replace the jokes with clear language that everyone can understand$qb$, $qb$Keep one joke but explain its meaning$qb$, $qb$Send separate explanations to colleagues who ask$qb$, $qb$Assume people will understand from context$qb$, $qb$Use more local jokes so the message feels friendly$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R7-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A coworker says you caused a delay, but the records show several factors contributed. The disagreement is now affecting a shared deadline. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Review the facts together, acknowledge your part, and agree what should change$qb$, $qb$Explain your part and ask them to review the other factors$qb$, $qb$Show the records to prove you were not mainly responsible$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to defend you$qb$, $qb$Blame the other factors instead$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R8-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate sends you a task request, but the deadline is missing. The request affects a second task already committed for the same day. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask for the deadline before planning the task$qb$, $qb$Start the task and ask about timing later$qb$, $qb$Assume it is due by the end of the week$qb$, $qb$Wait until the teammate sends another message$qb$, $qb$Ignore the request because it is incomplete$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R8-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague explains a problem and you are not sure you understood the main point. You will need to act independently after the discussion. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Repeat the main point in your own words and check it$qb$, $qb$Ask one question about the part that seems unclear$qb$, $qb$Take notes and review them after the discussion$qb$, $qb$Give your advice based on what you understood$qb$, $qb$Change the topic and return to it later$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R8-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate is unsure about using a new shared tracker. The colleague is not required to follow your preferred method. Which approach is most likely to gain support?$qb$, $qb$Show how it will reduce missed updates for the team$qb$, $qb$Explain its main features and ask them to try it$qb$, $qb$Tell them that most people already use it$qb$, $qb$Say that the manager prefers the new tracker$qb$, $qb$Keep using it yourself and hope they follow$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R8-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You and a colleague both need the same meeting room at the same time. Both people have valid needs and limited flexibility. What is the best way to resolve it?$qb$, $qb$Discuss which need is more time-sensitive and find a workable split$qb$, $qb$Check whether one meeting can move by a short time$qb$, $qb$Book the room first and let the colleague adjust$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to decide who gets the room$qb$, $qb$Cancel your meeting without discussing alternatives$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R8-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague seems confused by an idiom you used in a message. The message will be reused with colleagues from different backgrounds. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the point in simple, direct language$qb$, $qb$Explain what the idiom means in this case$qb$, $qb$Use a different idiom that may be easier$qb$, $qb$Ask another colleague to explain it$qb$, $qb$Continue using the same wording$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R8-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two teammates start blaming each other for a missed handoff. The disagreement is beginning to affect a shared deliverable. What is the best first step?$qb$, $qb$Bring the discussion back to what happened and what needs fixing$qb$, $qb$Let each person explain their view without interruption$qb$, $qb$Decide quickly who made the bigger mistake$qb$, $qb$Ask them to continue the discussion after work$qb$, $qb$Avoid getting involved unless the manager asks$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R9-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need to tell three teammates that a delivery date has changed. The change also affects another team's dependent work. Which message is clearest?$qb$, $qb$The delivery is now due Tuesday at 2 PM; please update your dependent tasks$qb$, $qb$The delivery has moved to Tuesday; please adjust your plans$qb$, $qb$The delivery date has changed; more details will follow$qb$, $qb$There may be a small timing change this week$qb$, $qb$Do not mention the change until someone asks$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
