  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R4-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague seems confused by an idiom you used in a message. The wording may become a shared standard across regions. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the point in simple, direct language$qb$, $qb$Explain what the idiom means in this case$qb$, $qb$Use a different idiom that may be easier$qb$, $qb$Ask another colleague to explain it$qb$, $qb$Continue using the same wording$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R4-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two teammates start blaming each other for a missed handoff. The conflict is now affecting team confidence and a delivery decision. What is the best first step?$qb$, $qb$Bring the discussion back to what happened and what needs fixing$qb$, $qb$Let each person explain their view without interruption$qb$, $qb$Decide quickly who made the bigger mistake$qb$, $qb$Ask them to continue the discussion after work$qb$, $qb$Avoid getting involved unless the manager asks$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R5-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Several teams will use your answer to coordinate work, and one assumption is still unresolved. Which communication strategy is most effective when briefing cross-departmental stakeholders on a complex workflow update?$qb$, $qb$Structuring the update around core operational outcomes, providing validated timeline milestones, and offering a summary sheet for quick reference.$qb$, $qb$Sending a detailed narrative document explaining every procedural step to ensure full technical transparency across all teams.$qb$, $qb$Delivering a brief verbal summary during a routine status call and taking note of any immediate questions raised.$qb$, $qb$Forwarding raw workflow documentation files to stakeholders and asking them to review relevant sections independently.$qb$, $qb$Delaying the briefing until all secondary project sub-tasks are fully completed to avoid sharing partial updates.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R5-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$The discussion includes facts, interpretations, and a proposed solution that may not address the main issue. What is the primary indicator of active listening during a feedback session with a key internal partner?$qb$, $qb$Paraphrasing the speaker's main points in your own words to verify mutual understanding before proposing solutions.$qb$, $qb$Taking exhaustive written notes continuously while the partner speaks to capture every spoken word.$qb$, $qb$Nodding attentively throughout the discussion and thanking the partner for sharing their perspective.$qb$, $qb$Offering immediate explanations for highlighted operational gaps to clarify context as soon as issues are raised.$qb$, $qb$Remaining silent throughout the conversation and waiting to address raised points in a follow-up email.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
