  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R10-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague misunderstands the phrase 'end of day' because your teams work in different time zones. The wording may become a shared reference across regions and functions. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Use a specific time and time zone in future messages$qb$, $qb$Clarify the exact time for this request$qb$, $qb$Use 'end of day' but add your city name$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague to convert the time themselves$qb$, $qb$Keep using the phrase because it is common at work$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R10-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate criticizes your work in a group message and you disagree with the criticism. The disagreement is affecting delivery, trust, and the wider team's behaviour. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the concern, check the facts, and discuss the issue without attacking the person$qb$, $qb$Ask for the specific example behind the criticism$qb$, $qb$Reply with evidence showing why they are wrong$qb$, $qb$Move the disagreement to a larger group$qb$, $qb$Ignore the message and stop cooperating$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R1-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need a colleague to correct a file before a client call. The communication will shape decisions across several functions, and some facts remain contested. Which message is best?$qb$, $qb$Please correct rows 12–15 by 2 PM and let me know when done; the 3 PM client call uses this file$qb$, $qb$Please correct rows 12–15 by 2 PM for the client call$qb$, $qb$Please correct the file before the client call$qb$, $qb$There are errors in the file that need attention$qb$, $qb$Please fix this urgently$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R1-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate explains a delay and mentions both a technical problem and a late input. You must determine what the stakeholder truly needs while separating evidence, assumptions, and advocacy. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Summarize both factors and ask which one is still blocking progress$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge both factors and ask what help is needed now$qb$, $qb$Focus on the technical problem because it sounds more serious$qb$, $qb$Tell them how to avoid delays next time$qb$, $qb$Assume the late input was the real cause$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R1-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You want a colleague to adopt a short review step before sending customer emails. You need commitment from senior peers with competing incentives and no direct reporting relationship. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Show an example of an avoidable error and how the review step would catch it$qb$, $qb$Explain the review step and ask them to try it for a week$qb$, $qb$Tell them careful people always review emails$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to add the step to their goals$qb$, $qb$Review their emails yourself instead$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R1-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague wants your draft today, but you need until tomorrow to check important figures. The trade-off affects strategic scope, delivery risk, cost, and commitments made to multiple stakeholders. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Explain the quality risk and agree whether they need an unchecked draft today or a checked version tomorrow$qb$, $qb$Offer an early draft today and a checked version tomorrow$qb$, $qb$Send the draft today without explaining the unchecked figures$qb$, $qb$Refuse to share anything until tomorrow$qb$, $qb$Promise a checked version today even though it is unrealistic$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R1-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A global team message uses several local jokes that some colleagues do not understand. The language may become an organization-wide reference used across cultures and professional groups. What is the best improvement?$qb$, $qb$Replace the jokes with clear language that everyone can understand$qb$, $qb$Keep one joke but explain its meaning$qb$, $qb$Send separate explanations to colleagues who ask$qb$, $qb$Assume people will understand from context$qb$, $qb$Use more local jokes so the message feels friendly$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R1-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A coworker says you caused a delay, but the records show several factors contributed. The conflict is polarizing teams and could damage a strategically important working relationship. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Review the facts together, acknowledge your part, and agree what should change$qb$, $qb$Explain your part and ask them to review the other factors$qb$, $qb$Show the records to prove you were not mainly responsible$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to defend you$qb$, $qb$Blame the other factors instead$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R2-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A customer asks when an issue will be fixed, but the exact time is not known. The message will influence decisions across functions, and different stakeholders currently hold different assumptions. Which reply is clearest?$qb$, $qb$We are checking the cause now; I will update you by 2 PM even if the fix is still in progress$qb$, $qb$We are working on it and I will give you another update by 2 PM$qb$, $qb$We expect progress today and will keep you informed$qb$, $qb$It should be fixed soon$qb$, $qb$We do not know$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R2-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague gives you instructions, then says, 'The last step is the most important.' You must determine the underlying need while separating evidence, interpretation, and the solution being advocated. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Confirm what the last step requires and why it matters before starting$qb$, $qb$Repeat the last step in your own words and check it$qb$, $qb$Write down the last step carefully$qb$, $qb$Focus only on the last step and ignore the earlier ones$qb$, $qb$Start immediately without checking your understanding$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R2-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You want a teammate to use a simple naming rule because files are often hard to find. You need commitment from senior peers whose incentives differ and whom you do not manage. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Show the time lost finding files and demonstrate how the naming rule makes retrieval easier$qb$, $qb$Explain the naming rule and ask them to try it on new files$qb$, $qb$Tell them organized teams use naming rules$qb$, $qb$Rename their files yourself$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to enforce the rule$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R2-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague needs a report today, but you can provide either a quick summary now or the full report tomorrow. The decision affects strategic scope, delivery risk, cost, and commitments already made elsewhere. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask what decision they need to make and agree which option best meets that need$qb$, $qb$Offer the summary today and full report tomorrow$qb$, $qb$Send the summary today without discussing their need$qb$, $qb$Refuse because the full report is not ready$qb$, $qb$Promise the full report today$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R2-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague says your email sounds too direct in their work culture. The wording may become an enterprise reference across cultures, functions, and levels of expertise. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask what wording felt too direct and adjust the message while keeping the action clear$qb$, $qb$Thank them and soften the wording where appropriate$qb$, $qb$Explain that you always write directly$qb$, $qb$Use very indirect language in all future emails$qb$, $qb$Ignore the comment because the meaning was clear$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R2-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two teammates disagree about a task and both keep repeating their positions. The conflict is polarizing groups and could damage a strategically important relationship if mishandled. What is the best next step?$qb$, $qb$Identify the shared outcome and compare the reasons behind each option$qb$, $qb$Summarize both positions and ask what each person needs$qb$, $qb$Ask them to vote on the two options$qb$, $qb$Choose the view of the more experienced teammate$qb$, $qb$End the discussion without a decision$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R3-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Several functions now depend on the answer, and leaders disagree about which uncertainty matters most. Which communication strategy is most effective when briefing cross-departmental stakeholders on a complex workflow update?$qb$, $qb$Structuring the update around core operational outcomes, providing validated timeline milestones, and offering a summary sheet for quick reference.$qb$, $qb$Sending a detailed narrative document explaining every procedural step to ensure full technical transparency across all teams.$qb$, $qb$Delivering a brief verbal summary during a routine status call and taking note of any immediate questions raised.$qb$, $qb$Forwarding raw workflow documentation files to stakeholders and asking them to review relevant sections independently.$qb$, $qb$Delaying the briefing until all secondary project sub-tasks are fully completed to avoid sharing partial updates.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R3-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$The speaker's proposed fix has executive support, but the evidence suggests the underlying problem may be different. What is the primary indicator of active listening during a feedback session with a key internal partner?$qb$, $qb$Paraphrasing the speaker's main points in your own words to verify mutual understanding before proposing solutions.$qb$, $qb$Taking exhaustive written notes continuously while the partner speaks to capture every spoken word.$qb$, $qb$Nodding attentively throughout the discussion and thanking the partner for sharing their perspective.$qb$, $qb$Offering immediate explanations for highlighted operational gaps to clarify context as soon as issues are raised.$qb$, $qb$Remaining silent throughout the conversation and waiting to address raised points in a follow-up email.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R3-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Adoption requires agreement from two influential peer groups with different incentives and no reporting line to you. How can a project coordinator best persuade reluctant peer leads to adopt a new inter-departmental request tool?$qb$, $qb$Demonstrating how the tool reduces administrative tracking effort for their specific teams and offering setup support.$qb$, $qb$Citing official management guidelines that mandate the adoption of standardized tracking software.$qb$, $qb$Highlighting the operational benefits that your own department will gain once all teams adopt the tool.$qb$, $qb$Asking department managers to issue formal reminders regarding compliance during upcoming team meetings.$qb$, $qb$Adopting the tool within your team only and waiting for peer leads to request access on their own schedule.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R3-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$When negotiating project scope adjustments with a client under tight budget limits, what represents the most effective approach The trade-off now affects a customer commitment, control requirement, and budget limit at the same time. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Evaluating core underlying priorities to rebalance deliverable scope while protecting project margin boundaries.$qb$, $qb$Offering a standard percentage price reduction immediately to demonstrate goodwill and secure contract agreement.$qb$, $qb$Reiterating standard rate card policies firmly and advising the client that adjustments are unavailable.$qb$, $qb$Escalating the negotiation directly to executive leadership to determine if margin exceptions can be granted.$qb$, $qb$Accepting all requested scope additions at current pricing to maintain positive client sentiment.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R3-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$The wording will be adopted across regions unless you resolve a difference in how two groups interpret it. What is the most effective method to ensure clear task execution when working with international remote collaborators?$qb$, $qb$Standardizing written communications with plain language, bulleted action items, and explicit verification check-ins.$qb$, $qb$Relying on frequent informal video calls to discuss task expectations and resolve misunderstandings verbally.$qb$, $qb$Sending automated task reminders at higher frequencies to ensure project deadlines remain top of mind.$qb$, $qb$Reassigning complex analytical tasks to local team members while leaving routine steps with global collaborators.$qb$, $qb$Using standard region-specific idioms and assuming collaborators will seek clarification if context is unclear.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R3-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$The dispute has split the wider team into camps and is beginning to delay a high-value decision. What is the recommended first step when resolving a resource allocation disagreement between two team members?$qb$, $qb$Facilitating a private alignment conversation to re-establish shared project goals and co-create clear task ownership.$qb$, $qb$Reassigning conflicting resources personally to eliminate immediate operational friction and protect project schedules.$qb$, $qb$Advising both team members to resolve their differences professionally before the upcoming project deadline.$qb$, $qb$Escalating the conflict to department managers so task responsibilities can be officially dictated.$qb$, $qb$Allocating contested resources strictly based on employee team seniority to settle the debate rapidly.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R4-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate sends you a task request, but the deadline is missing. Several functions need the update for different decisions, while two material facts remain disputed. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask for the deadline before planning the task$qb$, $qb$Start the task and ask about timing later$qb$, $qb$Assume it is due by the end of the week$qb$, $qb$Wait until the teammate sends another message$qb$, $qb$Ignore the request because it is incomplete$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R4-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague explains a problem and you are not sure you understood the main point. The stakeholder's preferred solution has momentum, but you need to determine whether it addresses the actual business problem. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Repeat the main point in your own words and check it$qb$, $qb$Ask one question about the part that seems unclear$qb$, $qb$Take notes and review them after the discussion$qb$, $qb$Give your advice based on what you understood$qb$, $qb$Change the topic and return to it later$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R4-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate is unsure about using a new shared tracker. You need sustained commitment from senior peers whose incentives conflict and whom you cannot direct. Which approach is most likely to gain support?$qb$, $qb$Show how it will reduce missed updates for the team$qb$, $qb$Explain its main features and ask them to try it$qb$, $qb$Tell them that most people already use it$qb$, $qb$Say that the manager prefers the new tracker$qb$, $qb$Keep using it yourself and hope they follow$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R4-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You and a colleague both need the same meeting room at the same time. The negotiation affects customer value, control exposure, budget, and commitments already made elsewhere. What is the best way to resolve it?$qb$, $qb$Discuss which need is more time-sensitive and find a workable split$qb$, $qb$Check whether one meeting can move by a short time$qb$, $qb$Book the room first and let the colleague adjust$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to decide who gets the room$qb$, $qb$Cancel your meeting without discussing alternatives$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R4-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague seems confused by an idiom you used in a message. The wording will be reused across regions and professional groups, where different interpretations could drive different decisions. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the point in simple, direct language$qb$, $qb$Explain what the idiom means in this case$qb$, $qb$Use a different idiom that may be easier$qb$, $qb$Ask another colleague to explain it$qb$, $qb$Continue using the same wording$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R4-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two teammates start blaming each other for a missed handoff. The dispute is splitting teams into camps and threatening a high-value cross-functional outcome. What is the best first step?$qb$, $qb$Bring the discussion back to what happened and what needs fixing$qb$, $qb$Let each person explain their view without interruption$qb$, $qb$Decide quickly who made the bigger mistake$qb$, $qb$Ask them to continue the discussion after work$qb$, $qb$Avoid getting involved unless the manager asks$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R5-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need to tell three teammates that a delivery date has changed. Several functions need the answer for different decisions, and material assumptions are still changing. Which message is clearest?$qb$, $qb$The delivery is now due Tuesday at 2 PM; please update your dependent tasks$qb$, $qb$The delivery has moved to Tuesday; please adjust your plans$qb$, $qb$The delivery date has changed; more details will follow$qb$, $qb$There may be a small timing change this week$qb$, $qb$Do not mention the change until someone asks$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R5-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$During a handover, a colleague gives several details and one part is unclear. The stakeholder's preferred solution is politically supported, but the evidence may point to a different underlying problem. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask about the unclear part and restate what you understood$qb$, $qb$Restate the main points and ask if anything important is missing$qb$, $qb$Write everything down and check it later$qb$, $qb$Let the colleague finish and make your own interpretation$qb$, $qb$Interrupt and ask them to start again$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R5-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You want a teammate to adopt a checklist that has reduced errors in your work. You need durable commitment from senior peers whose incentives conflict and whom you cannot direct. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Show the error reduction and invite them to try it on one task$qb$, $qb$Explain how the checklist works and share a copy$qb$, $qb$Tell them the checklist is now your preferred method$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to recommend the checklist to them$qb$, $qb$Keep the checklist to yourself because adoption is their choice$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R5-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks you to take an extra task, but your current deadline is at risk. The negotiation affects customer value, regulatory control, budget, and commitments already made elsewhere. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Explain your workload and agree which task should take priority$qb$, $qb$Offer to help with part of the task after your deadline$qb$, $qb$Accept the task and try to manage both$qb$, $qb$Refuse immediately because your schedule is full$qb$, $qb$Accept it but give it very little attention$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R5-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A remote colleague uses a phrase you do not understand. The wording may become an enterprise standard across regions, cultures, and professional groups. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask politely what the phrase means in this context$qb$, $qb$Repeat the phrase back and ask whether you understood correctly$qb$, $qb$Guess the meaning from the rest of the message$qb$, $qb$Ask another teammate what they think it means$qb$, $qb$Ignore the phrase and continue$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R5-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate sends an angry message about a mistake you did not make. The dispute is polarizing teams and threatens both a high-value outcome and a critical long-term relationship. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Reply calmly, clarify the facts, and focus on resolving the issue$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the frustration and ask to discuss the facts$qb$, $qb$Defend yourself immediately and explain who caused the mistake$qb$, $qb$Forward the message to your manager without replying$qb$, $qb$Send an equally strong message so your position is clear$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R6-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A manager asks, 'Will the file be ready today?' You know one check is still pending. Several functions will act on the update, but leaders disagree about which uncertainty is material. Which reply is best?$qb$, $qb$The file should be ready by 5 PM after I complete the final check; I will alert you if that changes$qb$, $qb$I expect to send it today after one remaining check$qb$, $qb$It is almost ready and should be sent today$qb$, $qb$Yes, it will probably be ready$qb$, $qb$Yes$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R6-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague says a task is 'not working' but gives no details. The proposed solution already has sponsorship, yet the evidence may indicate a different root problem. What is the best first response?$qb$, $qb$Ask what they expected, what happened, and when the problem started$qb$, $qb$Ask them to show you the problem$qb$, $qb$Suggest the most common fix$qb$, $qb$Tell them to try the task again$qb$, $qb$Assume you know what they mean$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R6-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You want your team to start recording one extra field because it will reduce follow-up questions. You need durable adoption from influential peers whose incentives conflict and whom you cannot direct. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Show the repeated follow-up problem and how the extra field would prevent it$qb$, $qb$Explain the new field and ask the team to try it$qb$, $qb$Tell the team the extra field is good practice$qb$, $qb$Add the field and tell everyone to use it$qb$, $qb$Record it yourself and leave others unchanged$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R6-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate needs your help this afternoon, but you have only 30 minutes free. The negotiation affects customer value, regulatory exposure, budget, and commitments already made elsewhere. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Agree what can realistically be achieved in 30 minutes and focus on that$qb$, $qb$Offer the 30 minutes and ask what is most urgent$qb$, $qb$Help until your next task becomes urgent$qb$, $qb$Say you are too busy to help$qb$, $qb$Promise more time than you have$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R6-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks you to explain a local abbreviation they have not seen before. The wording may become an enterprise reference across regions, cultures, and professional disciplines. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Explain the full term and what it means in this work context$qb$, $qb$Give the full form and a short example$qb$, $qb$Tell them everyone learns it after some time$qb$, $qb$Send them an old message that uses it$qb$, $qb$Tell them to search for it online$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R6-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You disagree with a teammate about who was responsible for an error. The dispute is polarizing groups and threatening both a strategic outcome and a long-term working relationship. What is the best way to move forward?$qb$, $qb$Check the facts, fix the immediate issue, and clarify responsibility without personal blame$qb$, $qb$Review what each person was expected to do$qb$, $qb$Explain why you believe the teammate was responsible$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to decide who was at fault$qb$, $qb$Stop cooperating until the issue is settled$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are handing over a task before leave. Several functions will act on your message, while senior leaders disagree about which unresolved fact should drive the decision. Which message gives the next person the clearest start?$qb$, $qb$The draft is in Folder X; Priya must confirm the price by noon, then send version 3 to the client$qb$, $qb$The draft is in Folder X; price confirmation is still needed before sending$qb$, $qb$The draft is nearly ready; please check the price and send it$qb$, $qb$Please finish the task while I am away$qb$, $qb$Everything is in the folder$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate explains why a deadline slipped, but you hear two different causes. The proposed solution already has influential support, but the evidence may point to a different underlying need. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Check that you understood both causes and ask which one had the larger impact$qb$, $qb$Summarize the two causes and ask one follow-up question$qb$, $qb$Focus on the cause that sounds most likely$qb$, $qb$Offer a solution before checking the details$qb$, $qb$Decide the teammate is making excuses$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You want a coworker to stop skipping a small check that prevents errors. You need lasting commitment from senior peers whose incentives conflict and whom you cannot direct. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Show a recent error the check would have caught and agree to use the check next time$qb$, $qb$Explain why the check matters and ask them to use it$qb$, $qb$Remind them that the process includes the check$qb$, $qb$Tell the manager that the coworker skips checks$qb$, $qb$Correct their work yourself whenever the check is missed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two colleagues need your help at the same time and both requests matter. The agreement affects customer value, control exposure, budget, and commitments already made across functions. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Clarify urgency and impact with both, then agree the order and timing$qb$, $qb$Tell both how much time you have and ask which can wait$qb$, $qb$Help the person who asked first$qb$, $qb$Choose the easier request so one is completed quickly$qb$, $qb$Promise both immediate help$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate misunderstands a message because the word 'soon' means different things to different people. The wording may become an enterprise reference across regions, cultures, and professional groups. What is the best improvement?$qb$, $qb$Replace 'soon' with a specific date or time$qb$, $qb$Explain what you meant by 'soon' in a follow-up$qb$, $qb$Use 'as soon as possible' next time$qb$, $qb$Ask the teammate to check whenever timing is unclear$qb$, $qb$Keep using 'soon' because it is common English$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A discussion about a missed task turns into comments about a person's attitude. The dispute is polarizing teams and threatening both a strategic outcome and an important long-term relationship. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Move the discussion back to the task, facts, impact, and next action$qb$, $qb$Ask everyone to describe what happened without personal comments$qb$, $qb$End the discussion and ask the manager to handle it$qb$, $qb$Defend the person being criticized$qb$, $qb$Allow the comments so people can express frustration$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need to correct a misunderstanding in a group chat. Several functions will act on your update, while influential leaders disagree about which unresolved fact is most material. Which message is best?$qb$, $qb$To clarify: the client call is Thursday at 11 AM, not Wednesday; please use the updated calendar invite$qb$, $qb$The client call is Thursday at 11 AM; the earlier message had the wrong day$qb$, $qb$Correction: the client call is Thursday$qb$, $qb$Please check the calendar because the timing changed$qb$, $qb$Ignore my earlier message$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague says they need 'more support' on a task. The proposed solution has strong sponsorship, but the evidence may indicate that the real business problem is different. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask what part is difficult and what kind of support would help$qb$, $qb$Ask them to describe where they are stuck$qb$, $qb$Offer to take over part of the task$qb$, $qb$Tell them to ask their manager for support$qb$, $qb$Assume they need more time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You want a teammate to start using a shared calendar for deadlines. You need durable commitment from senior peers whose incentives conflict and whom you cannot direct. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Show how missed dates have caused problems and how the calendar can prevent them$qb$, $qb$Explain how the shared calendar works and suggest a short trial$qb$, $qb$Tell them everyone should use the same calendar$qb$, $qb$Add their deadlines to the calendar yourself$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to make calendar use compulsory$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A coworker asks for your work by noon, but you can reliably deliver it at 2 PM. The agreement affects customer value, control exposure, budget, and commitments already made across functions. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Explain the constraint and agree whether 2 PM works or the scope should change$qb$, $qb$Offer 2 PM and ask what part is needed most urgently$qb$, $qb$Promise noon and try to finish faster$qb$, $qb$Refuse the request because noon is impossible$qb$, $qb$Say 'I'll try' without giving a realistic time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague misunderstands the phrase 'end of day' because your teams work in different time zones. The wording may become an enterprise reference across regions, cultures, and professional disciplines. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Use a specific time and time zone in future messages$qb$, $qb$Clarify the exact time for this request$qb$, $qb$Use 'end of day' but add your city name$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague to convert the time themselves$qb$, $qb$Keep using the phrase because it is common at work$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate criticizes your work in a group message and you disagree with the criticism. The dispute is polarizing groups and threatening both a strategic outcome and a long-term cross-functional relationship. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the concern, check the facts, and discuss the issue without attacking the person$qb$, $qb$Ask for the specific example behind the criticism$qb$, $qb$Reply with evidence showing why they are wrong$qb$, $qb$Move the disagreement to a larger group$qb$, $qb$Ignore the message and stop cooperating$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A customer asks when an issue will be fixed, but the exact time is not known. Multiple functions will act on the answer and leaders currently disagree about the material uncertainty. Which reply is clearest?$qb$, $qb$We are checking the cause now; I will update you by 2 PM even if the fix is still in progress$qb$, $qb$We are working on it and I will give you another update by 2 PM$qb$, $qb$We expect progress today and will keep you informed$qb$, $qb$It should be fixed soon$qb$, $qb$We do not know$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague gives you instructions, then says, 'The last step is the most important.' The stated solution has strong sponsorship, but evidence suggests the underlying need may be different. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Confirm what the last step requires and why it matters before starting$qb$, $qb$Repeat the last step in your own words and check it$qb$, $qb$Write down the last step carefully$qb$, $qb$Focus only on the last step and ignore the earlier ones$qb$, $qb$Start immediately without checking your understanding$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You want a teammate to use a simple naming rule because files are often hard to find. Durable adoption requires agreement from senior peers with competing incentives and no reporting line to you. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Show the time lost finding files and demonstrate how the naming rule makes retrieval easier$qb$, $qb$Explain the naming rule and ask them to try it on new files$qb$, $qb$Tell them organized teams use naming rules$qb$, $qb$Rename their files yourself$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to enforce the rule$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague needs a report today, but you can provide either a quick summary now or the full report tomorrow. The trade-off affects customer value, control exposure, budget, and commitments already made elsewhere. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask what decision they need to make and agree which option best meets that need$qb$, $qb$Offer the summary today and full report tomorrow$qb$, $qb$Send the summary today without discussing their need$qb$, $qb$Refuse because the full report is not ready$qb$, $qb$Promise the full report today$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague says your email sounds too direct in their work culture. The wording may become an enterprise reference across regions, cultures, and professional disciplines. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask what wording felt too direct and adjust the message while keeping the action clear$qb$, $qb$Thank them and soften the wording where appropriate$qb$, $qb$Explain that you always write directly$qb$, $qb$Use very indirect language in all future emails$qb$, $qb$Ignore the comment because the meaning was clear$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two teammates disagree about a task and both keep repeating their positions. The dispute is polarizing groups and threatening a strategic outcome and a long-term working relationship. What is the best next step?$qb$, $qb$Identify the shared outcome and compare the reasons behind each option$qb$, $qb$Summarize both positions and ask what each person needs$qb$, $qb$Ask them to vote on the two options$qb$, $qb$Choose the view of the more experienced teammate$qb$, $qb$End the discussion without a decision$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A business unit asks for an immediate public statement after a service disruption, but facts are still changing and Legal has not confirmed whether customer data was affected. What should you communicate now?$qb$, $qb$State what is confirmed, what remains under investigation, when the next update will come, and avoid claims that may later prove false.$qb$, $qb$Share the confirmed operational facts and say that further details will follow after review.$qb$, $qb$Use the most likely explanation but label it as provisional so stakeholders have something useful.$qb$, $qb$Delay all communication until every material fact is verified.$qb$, $qb$Reassure customers that no serious impact occurred unless evidence proves otherwise.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A senior colleague gives a long explanation for why their team missed a commitment. You hear three possible causes, but they appear most concerned about one hidden dependency. What is the best next move?$qb$, $qb$Reflect the main themes, test whether the dependency is the real constraint, and let them correct your interpretation before moving to solutions.$qb$, $qb$Summarize the three causes and ask which one they want help with first.$qb$, $qb$Focus on the dependency because it appears to be the most important factor.$qb$, $qb$Offer a solution for the first cause so the conversation becomes more practical.$qb$, $qb$End the discussion and ask them to send a written root-cause analysis.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new operating model will reduce duplication but requires two respected leaders to give up decision rights. Both privately agree with the logic but fear losing influence. What is the strongest way to build support?$qb$, $qb$Connect the change to shared outcomes, address status and accountability concerns directly, and involve them in shaping the future decision model.$qb$, $qb$Show the efficiency gains and ask them to publicly endorse the proposal.$qb$, $qb$Secure the chief executive's backing before asking either leader to commit.$qb$, $qb$Offer each leader a different title so the loss of authority feels smaller.$qb$, $qb$Present the redesign as non-negotiable to avoid prolonged resistance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are renegotiating a strategic contract. The supplier can lower unit price, improve service levels, or absorb more demand risk, but not all three. What should guide your trade-off?$qb$, $qb$Prioritize the combination that creates the best total value against business objectives, risk exposure, and likely future demand.$qb$, $qb$Choose the lowest unit price because it is the easiest benefit to quantify.$qb$, $qb$Split the concessions evenly across price, service, and risk so both sides compromise.$qb$, $qb$Ask the supplier which concession is least costly for them and accept that one.$qb$, $qb$Delay the negotiation until you can secure all three concessions.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A regional team says a global instruction is clear in English but culturally reads as accusatory in their market. The factual requirement must not change. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Preserve the required meaning while adapting tone and examples locally, then validate that the translated version cannot be misread.$qb$, $qb$Use a professional translation service and keep the original wording as closely as possible.$qb$, $qb$Let the region rewrite the instruction entirely because they understand the local culture better.$qb$, $qb$Keep the global wording unchanged so every country receives identical language.$qb$, $qb$Remove the sensitive sentence from the regional version to avoid offense.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two functions are escalating a recurring dispute over who owns customer remediation. Both can cite process documents supporting their position. What should you do first?$qb$, $qb$Establish the customer outcome and current decision rights, identify where the documents conflict, and agree an interim ownership path before fixing governance.$qb$, $qb$Ask both functions to nominate one leader to negotiate a compromise.$qb$, $qb$Choose the interpretation supported by the most recently issued document.$qb$, $qb$Escalate ownership to the executive committee because the documents conflict.$qb$, $qb$Let both functions continue handling cases independently until policy is clarified.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A restructuring must be announced before all role impacts are known, and rumours are driving resignations. What is the strongest approach?$qb$, $qb$State what is decided, what remains open, why the change is occurring, how decisions will be made, and when people will hear more.$qb$, $qb$Share confirmed decisions and a timetable, but avoid unresolved issues.$qb$, $qb$Wait until most individual impacts are known before communicating.$qb$, $qb$Give managers a script and let them decide what to disclose.$qb$, $qb$Reassure employees disruption will be limited despite the uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A country leader keeps changing objections to a global operating model whenever solutions are offered. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Explore the interests and constraints beneath the objections, confirm them, then separate legitimate local needs from positional resistance.$qb$, $qb$Ask for a complete written list of objections and answer each formally.$qb$, $qb$Offer more local flexibility until the leader supports the model.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the inconsistent objections to the leader's manager.$qb$, $qb$Implement the approved model without further discussion.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A committee favors projects with clear near-term returns, while a strategically important capability has uncertain financial benefits. How should you make the case?$qb$, $qb$Link it to agreed strategic outcomes, quantify what is defensible, expose uncertainty, and compare consequences of funding versus not funding it.$qb$, $qb$Emphasize competitor activity and request a modest initial budget.$qb$, $qb$Focus mainly on qualitative strategic benefits.$qb$, $qb$Secure the CEO's endorsement before the committee meets.$qb$, $qb$Present an optimistic return case so it competes on equal terms.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two business units both want control of a shared platform because future revenue allocation depends on ownership. What is the best negotiating basis?$qb$, $qb$Separate governance, economics and operating responsibilities, identify shared interests, and align decision rights with enterprise value and risk.$qb$, $qb$Split ownership equally so neither side dominates.$qb$, $qb$Give control to the unit contributing most current revenue.$qb$, $qb$Ask the executive committee to select an owner first.$qb$, $qb$Let both units control the platform independently in their markets.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A globally valid policy sounds accusatory when translated directly into one region's language and may suppress reporting. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Preserve intent and controls while adapting wording and examples with local expertise, then verify equivalent meaning.$qb$, $qb$Use the direct translation because wording must be consistent.$qb$, $qb$Let the regional team rewrite it without central constraints.$qb$, $qb$Add a disclaimer explaining the wording may sound unusually strong.$qb$, $qb$Keep only the English version authoritative.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two vice presidents have turned a resource dispute into personal criticism, and their teams are copying the hostility. What intervention is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Stop personal escalation, define the enterprise decision required, surface interests and evidence, and agree both the resource choice and behavioral expectations.$qb$, $qb$Meet them separately and decide whose resource case is stronger.$qb$, $qb$Ask their common executive to impose a resource split.$qb$, $qb$Move the resources temporarily to a neutral function.$qb$, $qb$Allow the two leaders to resolve the issue themselves.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A board update must explain why a profitable division is losing strategic customers although headline KPIs remain strong. What is the strongest approach?$qb$, $qb$Lead with the contradiction, isolate decision-relevant evidence, distinguish fact from interpretation, and state the choices now required.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate issue with a reasonable local action, but leave the broader assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather some additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask the affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or externally forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A long-serving executive calls a governance change 'unworkable' but becomes quiet when asked for alternatives. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Explore the failure they foresee and the experience behind it, then test your understanding before debating solutions.$qb$, $qb$Take a practical partial step and escalate the remaining uncertainty to a higher authority.$qb$, $qb$Apply the existing policy more carefully, even though it does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects the most visible short-term target.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the conflicting evidence and continue with the established position.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Business presidents must adopt a shared data standard that creates enterprise value but adds local cost. What is most likely to win durable support?$qb$, $qb$Make enterprise and local economics visible, acknowledge who bears cost, co-design guardrails, and align incentives with the shared outcome.$qb$, $qb$Act on the strongest available signal while keeping a later review point.$qb$, $qb$Seek broader consensus before making a limited decision.$qb$, $qb$Use a familiar precedent even though important conditions differ.$qb$, $qb$Choose the easiest path to implement regardless of longer-term consequences.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategic supplier can meet an urgent capacity need only with five-year exclusivity, while demand after year two is uncertain. What is the strongest move?$qb$, $qb$Trade across capacity, duration, volume bands, exit rights and pricing so near-term certainty does not create disproportionate lock-in.$qb$, $qb$Resolve the immediate pressure and document the remaining risk for later review.$qb$, $qb$Use a narrower solution that improves one dimension but leaves key dependencies unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Transfer the decision to another function without clarifying enterprise ownership.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current plan even when evidence shows its assumptions are weakening.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A global employee survey phrase becomes a serious accusation when translated in several countries, depressing response rates. What is the best correction?$qb$, $qb$Preserve intended meaning, use culturally validated wording, and test equivalence before comparing results across regions.$qb$, $qb$Introduce a sensible safeguard but leave the underlying incentives or decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Run a small corrective action focused mainly on the most visible symptom.$qb$, $qb$Ask individual managers to compensate for the problem through extra effort.$qb$, $qb$Treat the issue as unavoidable and make no material change.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two division heads agree on strategy but repeatedly clash over who controls customer decisions. What should their common leader do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify enterprise decision rights across the customer journey, surface interests driving the dispute, and resolve both interface and behavioral expectations.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate issue with a reasonable local action, but leave the broader assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather some additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask the affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or externally forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A board paper is technically correct, but directors interpret its implications differently. What should you do before the vote?$qb$, $qb$Reframe it around the decision, consequences, assumptions and unresolved choices, then verify a shared interpretation.$qb$, $qb$Address the most urgent part of the issue and schedule the broader questions for a later review.$qb$, $qb$Use an existing policy or precedent even though it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the decision upward without first clarifying the trade-offs.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects current targets regardless of wider consequences.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A regional president says, 'I can live with the plan,' but their tone suggests unresolved concern. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Explore what remains difficult, reflect it accurately, and distinguish reluctant commitment from an unresolved risk.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core design and incentives intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask the main stakeholders for more input but do not establish how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term decision primarily to restore speed.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the status quo because changing it could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Country leaders must change practices that help them hit local targets but undermine an enterprise transformation. How should you build support?$qb$, $qb$Expose the incentive conflict, connect the change to shared outcomes, co-design trade-offs, and align measures with the new behavior.$qb$, $qb$Take a reasonable immediate action on the visible issue, but leave the wider assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or external pressure forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A joint-venture partner wants guaranteed purchases while you need flexibility because demand is uncertain. What structure is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Use tiered commitments, review points and reciprocal protections so neither party absorbs disproportionate demand risk.$qb$, $qb$Address the most urgent part of the issue and schedule the broader questions for a later review.$qb$, $qb$Use an existing policy or precedent even though it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the decision upward without first clarifying the trade-offs.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects current targets regardless of wider consequences.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A global town hall favors rapid public questions, but some regions engage far more in smaller forums. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Offer multiple equivalent participation channels and ensure important input reaches decisions without privileging one communication style.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core design and incentives intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask the main stakeholders for more input but do not establish how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term decision primarily to restore speed.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the status quo because changing it could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two executives fight over whether to compensate a failed strategic account, with one fearing precedent. What should their leader do?$qb$, $qb$Separate the urgent customer decision from policy precedent, establish shared criteria, and resolve both without personalizing ownership.$qb$, $qb$Take a reasonable immediate action on the visible issue, but leave the wider assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or external pressure forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategic announcement must explain a painful portfolio exit while protecting confidence in the remaining businesses. What should the executive message do?$qb$, $qb$Explain the decision logic, consequences, uncertainties and next commitments candidly, linking the exit to the future portfolio without minimizing affected stakeholders.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate concern with a sensible local action, then revisit the wider implications at the next formal review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more input and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central ownership or trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current design and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until external evidence makes the problem indisputable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$In a capital review, a quiet executive repeatedly asks narrow questions about liquidity while others debate growth. What is the strongest response?$qb$, $qb$Explore what risk their questions are pointing to, reflect it back, and test whether the group is overlooking a constraint before returning to the growth debate.$qb$, $qb$Use the closest existing policy or precedent and add a modest safeguard for this case.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a senior decision without first clarifying the competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest option to restore momentum, even if wider consequences remain unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position and leave secondary impacts to other owners.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A shared-service redesign needs powerful business leaders to give up bespoke processes they value. What is the strongest influence approach?$qb$, $qb$Show the enterprise and local trade-offs, involve leaders in defining essential exceptions, and align incentives and governance so standardization creates value they can defend.$qb$, $qb$Improve the existing approach incrementally while leaving its main incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask stakeholders for additional views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term compromise that reduces visible tension without addressing the underlying mechanism.$qb$, $qb$Continue the status quo because changing it could introduce new uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A potential alliance partner wants broad intellectual-property rights in return for access to a market you cannot enter alone. What is the strongest negotiation approach?$qb$, $qb$Separate access, ownership, fields of use, duration and exit rights, then construct exchanges that protect strategic options while giving the partner sufficient value.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate concern with a sensible local action, then revisit the wider implications at the next formal review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more input and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central ownership or trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current design and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until external evidence makes the problem indisputable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A global performance process rewards employees who openly challenge senior leaders, but in some cultures disagreement is expressed indirectly. What should leadership change?$qb$, $qb$Assess the underlying quality of challenge through multiple observable behaviors and channels rather than treating one culturally specific expression as the standard.$qb$, $qb$Use the closest existing policy or precedent and add a modest safeguard for this case.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a senior decision without first clarifying the competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest option to restore momentum, even if wider consequences remain unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position and leave secondary impacts to other owners.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two executives dispute which function should own a new capability, and both have credible arguments. What should their leader do?$qb$, $qb$Decide from the capability's end-to-end outcomes, dependencies and decision rights, while addressing incentives that make organizational ownership a prize rather than a means.$qb$, $qb$Improve the existing approach incrementally while leaving its main incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask stakeholders for additional views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term compromise that reduces visible tension without addressing the underlying mechanism.$qb$, $qb$Continue the status quo because changing it could introduce new uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A major strategic reset will close one growth initiative and double investment in another. Both teams have strong internal supporters. How should you present the decision?$qb$, $qb$Explain the common decision criteria, evidence, trade-offs and implications for both choices, while acknowledging uncertainty and specifying what happens next.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, take a useful immediate step on the main issue and schedule the broader structural question for a later governance review.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, gather additional evidence and make a limited adjustment, but leave the central trade-off or ownership unresolved.$qb$, $qb$At first, preserve the current framework and ask the affected leaders to absorb or manage its consequences.$qb$, $qb$In this case, maintain the existing course until the risk becomes certain enough to force a decision.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
