  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R06-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A board-approved strategy requires closing a respected legacy institution within the group. Employees identify strongly with it and external stakeholders may see closure as abandoning the company's heritage. How should the CEO communicate?$qb$, $qb$Explain the strategic necessity and evidence, honor the institution's real contribution, distinguish heritage worth preserving from structures that no longer serve the future, and make transition commitments concrete.$qb$, $qb$Take a credible immediate action on the visible issue and defer broader institutional implications to the next governance cycle.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central enterprise trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences within their areas.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until external events make change unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R06-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$In a capital-allocation discussion, a director repeatedly asks whether a small supplier ecosystem can survive your proposed consolidation. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Explore whether ecosystem health is a strategic dependency or source of resilience, assess the consequence of losing it, and incorporate that evidence before finalizing consolidation.$qb$, $qb$Apply the closest existing precedent with added safeguards, although it does not fully address the strategic context.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position even if material risk is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
